# Bar / meter

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A bar or meter shows how full something is on a scale that people can see the ends of, such as disk space used or battery charge. It is not for progress on a task that is still running. Use the browser’s `<progress>` element for that.

This page owns the `<meter>` element and Home Assistant’s `ha-bar`. For gauges and charts, see [Energy flow and other chart families](../home-assistant/energy-flow-and-other-charts.html).

This page covers:

- Bar / meter
  - `<meter>`
  - `ha-bar`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

Both are a flat track with a filled part. A label names what is measured, and the value is written beside or under it, with its unit and range.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-meter"><code>&lt;meter&gt;</code></h3>

The browser’s own meter for a value inside a known range. Its attributes are `value`, `min` (0), `max` (1), `low`, `high` and `optimum`. The last three split the scale into good, middling and poor regions, and the browser colours them.

It has a role of `meter`, and browsers pass on its value, minimum and maximum. Give it a name with a `<label for>` or `aria-label`. A browser may read only the number, so write the reading in text next to it, with its unit.

```html
<label for="battery">Backup battery charge</label>
<meter id="battery" min="0" max="100" value="45">45%</meter>
<p>Reported charge: 45% on a scale from 0% to 100%.</p>
```

The browser draws the meter and sets its colours, which differ between browsers. The Civica theme cannot change them. Home Assistant does not use `<meter>` in its own screens. A dashboard author cannot add one to an ordinary card.

<h3 id="child-ha-bar"><code>ha-bar</code></h3>

Home Assistant’s bar, used for storage and system load in its settings screens, and inside its metric display. It has `min` (0), `max` (100) and `value` properties. It draws a track and a fill in a picture, 12 pixels high and the full width. It has no role, no value and no text, so a screen reader does not announce it. It is not a card, and you cannot add it from YAML.

Variables: `--ha-bar-background-color` (the track, default the secondary background colour), `--ha-bar-primary-color` (the fill, default the primary colour) and `--ha-bar-border-radius` (default the small border radius). The Civica theme sets the two colours it relies on: `primary-color` (#1D70B8 in light mode, #C0D7EB in dark mode) and `secondary-background-color` (#F3F2F1, #30363A). It sets no `ha-bar` variable and no small border radius, so the corners stay as Home Assistant draws them.

Home Assistant’s metric display changes the fill to the warning colour above 50% and the error colour above 85%. It always prints the percentage as text. The Civica theme sets those colours, `warning-color` and `error-color`. The settings storage page uses 85 and 95%.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- `<meter>` has a role, a name and a value, which `ha-bar` lacks.
- Browser meter colours cannot be themed.
- `ha-bar` is a picture only, so the written value does the work.
- The track is a pale grey, and on a white page it calculates at about 1.12 to 1, so the bar’s edge is hard to see. The fill and the text carry the meaning.
- A dashboard author cannot place either of them on a card.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Label every bar. Write the value with its unit and range, such as “62% of 64 GB”. Say what “high” means, and explain any warning level in words. Do not use red, amber and green alone.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.1.1 Non-text Content and 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: label and value in text.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: a warning level is also written.
- 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: check the fill and track against the background in your own theme.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: `<meter>` has them; `ha-bar` does not.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: the bar is full width, and stays 12 pixels high.

